          "slug": "introduction",
          "title": "Introduction",
          "description": "What XENO RT is, and where it fits in the XENO ecosystem.",
          "body": "# Introduction\n\n**XENO RT** (`xrt`) is a from-scratch inference runtime written in pure Rust. It loads GGUF language models with memory-mapped, zero-copy startup, runs them on optimized CPU kernels or a CUDA GPU backend, and serves them behind an **OpenAI-compatible HTTP API** — so anything that talks to OpenAI can talk to a fully local model instead.\n\nIt is positioned as a memory-safe alternative to llama.cpp, prioritizing clean architecture and first-class GGUF compatibility while delivering competitive speed through hand-tuned kernels.\n\n## What it does\n\n- **Runs GGUF models locally** — Llama and Qwen family architectures, loaded straight from a `.gguf` file or the HuggingFace Hub.\n- **Speaks OpenAI** — a drop-in `/v1/chat/completions`, `/v1/completions`, and `/v1/models` server with SSE streaming.\n- **CPU or CUDA** — optimized CPU kernels everywhere, with a feature-gated CUDA-resident backend for GPU acceleration.\n- **Agent-aware KV cache** — a paged key-value cache with an experimental *agent-adaptive* mode that keeps hot tokens in full precision and quantizes the rest.\n- **More than text** — an image-domain endpoint (background removal) and multimodal image inputs when a vision projector is loaded.\n- **Embeddable** — a C ABI and a Python module so other languages can drive the runtime in-process.\n\n## Where it fits\n\nXENO RT is **Layer 2 (Native Engine & Inference)** of the XENO ecosystem. It is the unified local-inference runtime that lets the whole platform work offline: the [XENO Agent SDK](/docs/agent-sdk) and [XENO Agent CLI](/docs/agent-cli) can route reasoning through `xrt` instead of the cloud, and creative apps use it for on-device AI. Because the API is OpenAI-compatible, switching an app between the XENO cloud and a local `xrt` server is a base-URL change.\n\n## Maturity\n\nXENO RT is **beta** (Rust, v0.2.0). The core inference path — GGUF loading, CPU kernels, sampling, the OpenAI server, HuggingFace downloads — is the most mature part and is covered by tests. GPU acceleration, the quantized/adaptive KV cache modes, multimodal, and the vision endpoint are newer; this documentation calls out where a feature is experimental or backend-specific. It is licensed under **Apache-2.0**.\n\n## Next steps\n\n- [Installation](/docs/rt/installation) — build `xrt` from source\n- [Quickstart](/docs/rt/quickstart) — download a model, generate text, start the server\n- [Architecture](/docs/rt/architecture) — the crate map and how a request flows"
